(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the twelve-tense-aspect matrix of the English verbal system and evaluate its communicative precision in scientific discourse.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Theoretical Matrix of Tense and Aspect:) Tj
T*
(   English verbal syntax is structured around two morphological tenses \(Past, Present; future) Tj
T*
(   marked periphrastically with modals\) and two aspects:) Tj
T*
(   - Perfect Aspect: Indicates anteriority or completion relative to a reference point.) Tj
T*
(   - Progressive \(Continuous\) Aspect: Denotes ongoing, temporary, or uncompleted processes.) Tj
T*
(   Combining these yields the twelve canonical categories:) Tj
T*
(   - Present Simple, Present Continuous, Present Perfect, Present Perfect Continuous.) Tj
T*
(   - Past Simple, Past Continuous, Past Perfect, Past Perfect Continuous.) Tj
T*
(   - Future Simple, Future Continuous, Future Perfect, Future Perfect Continuous.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Communicative Precision in Scientific Manuscripts:) Tj
T*
(   - Present Simple: Codifies timeless empirical facts, universal biological laws, and permanent) Tj
T*
(   properties \(e.g., "Enzymes lower activation energy"\).) Tj
T*
(   - Past Simple: Records discrete, completed historical experiments and methodological executions) Tj
T*
(   \(e.g., "We centrifuged the lysate at 10,000 g"\).) Tj
T*
(   - Present Perfect: Synthesizes cumulative scholarly consensus and bridges historical literature) Tj
T*
(   with current research frontiers \(e.g., "Numerous researchers have demonstrated the efficacy of) Tj
T*
(   CRISPR"\).) Tj
T*
(   - Past Perfect: Decouples antecedent methodological events from subsequent occurrences,) Tj
T*
(   preventing temporal ambiguity in procedural descriptions.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the conventional tense shifts across the IMRaD sections \(Introduction, Methods, Results, Discussion\) of research papers.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The IMRaD Macrostructure Tense Conventions:) Tj
T*
(   Scientific research articles require systematic tense shifting across sections to mirror the) Tj
T*
(   epistemological status of the data:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Section-by-Section Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   - Introduction Section:) Tj
T*
(   - General Background / Established Knowledge: Present Simple \("Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune) Tj
T*
(   disease"\).) Tj
T*
(   - Literature Review: Present Perfect \("Recent studies have implicated environmental factors"\)) Tj
T*
(   or Past Simple when citing specific authors/dates \("Smith et al. \(2021\) identified three) Tj
T*
(   candidate loci"\).) Tj
T*
(   - Research Objective / Paper Blueprint: Present Simple \("In this study, we evaluate..."\) or) Tj
T*
(   Past Simple \("Our objective was to evaluate..."\).) Tj
T*
(   - Materials and Methods Section:) Tj
T*
(   - Almost exclusively Past Simple \(Passive or Active\): "Cells were cultured", "The plasmid was) Tj
T*
(   digested", "We quantified protein concentration".) Tj
T*
(   - Results Section:) Tj
T*
(   - Past Simple for reporting specific experimental findings: "Optical density increased) Tj
T*
(   linearly", "The knockout strain failed to grow".) Tj
T*
(   - Present Simple when referencing visual graphics: "Figure 1 illustrates the dose-response) Tj
T*
(   curve".) Tj
T*
(   - Discussion Section:) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
