(   Standard conditionals match temporal frameworks \(Past-to-Past, Present-to-Future\). Mixed) Tj
T*
(   conditionals bridge two distinct time frameworks, most commonly pairing a past counterfactual) Tj
T*
(   condition with an ongoing present result.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Type A Mixed Conditional \(Past Condition -> Present Result\):) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: If + [Past Perfect], ... [Would + Base Verb \(Present\)].) Tj
T*
(   - Diagnostic Example in Biotechnology:) Tj
T*
(   - Context: The lab failed to preserve an aliquot of a unique mutant strain in liquid nitrogen) Tj
T*
(   last year. Today, that strain is extinct.) Tj
T*
(   - Mixed Formulation: "If the researchers had cryopreserved the hybridoma line in 2023, the) Tj
T*
(   monoclonal antibody would be available for our current clinical trials.") Tj
T*
(   - Analysis: The condition is in the past \('had cryopreserved'\), but the hypothetical) Tj
T*
(   consequence operates in the present \('would be available'\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The Type B Mixed Conditional \(Permanent Present Quality -> Past Consequence\):) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: If + [Past Simple / Subjunctive Were], ... [Would have + Past Participle].) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "If the bacteriophage were not so lethal to Salmonella, the treatment would have) Tj
T*
(   failed during the outbreak.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
