12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the Future-in-the-Past construction \('would' / 'was going to'\) in documenting historical research trajectories.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition and Structural Role:) Tj
T*
(   Future-in-the-Past expresses an event or intention that was prospective relative to a past) Tj
T*
(   reference point:) Tj
T*
(   - Forms: 'would + base verb', 'was/were going to + base verb', 'was/were to + base verb'.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Usage in Scientific Historical Retrospectives and Discussion Sections:) Tj
T*
(   Essential when narrating the trajectory of experimental campaigns or chronicling unexpected) Tj
T*
(   breakthroughs:) Tj
T*
(   - Example 1: "When Avery, MacLeod, and McCarty published their 1944 findings, they could not) Tj
T*
(   anticipate that their discovery would launch the modern era of molecular genetics.") Tj
T*
(   - Example 2: "The initial clinical trial was designed to evaluate tolerability; subsequent) Tj
T*
(   investigations were to assess therapeutic efficacy.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Avoiding Colloquialisms:) Tj
T*
(   In formal research manuscripts, prefer 'would + verb' or 'was destined to' over informal) Tj
T*
(   conversational phrasings like 'was gonna'.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
